---
// La pagina che Apple pretende per pubblicare un'app. Il testo viene dai
// documenti veri dei due progetti, non da un modello generico: e' per questo
// che dice cose diverse per ciascuno dei motori di Minta invece di una riga
// sola buona per tutto.
//
// La parte di Minta segue l'informativa approvata dal proprietario, versione
// inglese: MintaApp/Documentation/INFORMATIVA-PRIVACY-BOZZA.md (dal 18 ottobre
// 2026 la versione breve, Lotto 26 dell'app). Se cambia quella, cambia questa
// (e i testi nell'app: foglio del consenso e Impostazioni › Privacy).
// Ogni motore e' un blocco .motore a se': si toglie intero, senza toccare il
// resto. Titolare e contatto stanno in cima alla pagina, minori e modifiche in
// fondo: valgono per tutte e due le app.
//
// 27 ottobre 2026: allineata alla bozza dopo i Lotti 39, 42, 53, 69 e 75 dell'app
// (avanzamento delle serie, niente parte senza un tocco, paese dell'App Store,
// Private Cloud Compute senza domanda, trascrizione solo sul telefono). La terza
// app, l'esperimento con Claude Code, e' uscita dal sito, e con lei il suo paragrafo.
//
// La versione italiana e' privacy/it.astro (decisione del proprietario del
// 19 ottobre 2026: il titolare e' in Italia). Le due pagine dicono le stesse
// cose: se ne cambi una, cambia anche l'altra. Questa resta all'indirizzo
// privacy/ perche' l'app e App Store Connect puntano qui.
//
// Le note interne, le verifiche e il ragionamento sul ruolo di titolare
// restano nei documenti privati del proprietario: questo repository e' pubblico.
//
// ATTENZIONE a «Groq keeps nothing»: e' vera (Zero Data Retention) finche'
// quell'interruttore resta acceso nella console di Groq, e nessun test di
// questo sito puo' accorgersi se viene spento. Se tocchi le impostazioni di
// Groq, rileggi questa pagina.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import '../styles/informativa.css';

const aggiornata = '8 November 2026';
---

<Base
  titolo="Privacy — TROVA.ME"
  descrizione="What each TROVA.ME app does with your data, in plain terms. Minta keeps your recordings on your iPhone; they leave only for a job you ask for."
>
  <div class="contenitore informativa">
    <nav class="lingue" aria-label="Language">
      <span aria-current="page">English</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <a href={path('privacy/it/')} lang="it" hreflang="it">Italiano</a>
    </nav>

    <h1>Privacy</h1>

    <p class="apertura">
      TROVA.ME makes iPhone apps. This page says what each app does with your data, in plain terms.
      It is short because the apps collect very little.
    </p>

    <p>
      The data controller is <strong>Daniele Riccarand</strong>, acting as an individual, in
      Milan, Italy. For anything on this page, write to <a href="mailto:dan@trova.me"
        >dan@trova.me</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This site has no analytics, no cookies, no trackers and no contact form. Nothing you do here
      is recorded. TROVA.ME neither receives nor stores the logs of the service that hosts the site.
    </p>

    <h2>Minta</h2>
    <p>
      Minta records audio, transcribes it, and writes up the transcript. <strong>In short:
      Minta keeps nothing of your recordings.</strong> They stay on your iPhone (and in your
      iCloud, if you turn on sync). They leave only for a job you ask for (to Minta Cloud after
      your yes), and Minta Cloud is only a passage. No ads, analytics or tracking.
    </p>

    <h3>On your iPhone</h3>
    <p>
      Recordings, transcriptions, write-ups, notes and your glossary stay on the phone until you
      delete them, and can be part of your device backups. Your Minta Cloud sign-in is in the iOS
      encrypted keychain. These also stay on the phone:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>speaker recognition</strong> (off until you turn it on): it tells the voices
        apart (A, B, C…) with a model on the phone; no voice prints, no recognizing who people
        are. You type the voices’ names and they stay with the recording;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spotlight</strong>: the name, date and first sentence of the write-up go into
        the iPhone’s search (it can be turned off in Settings);
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Export</strong>: the .minta file (audio and texts) or the audio copy goes
        only where you send it; a podcast’s audio stays in Minta.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>When data leaves</h3>
    <p>
      Only for a job you ask for, and nothing goes to Minta Cloud without your yes: the first time,
      Minta says what leaves and where it goes, and asks your permission. The instructions of the
      type of write-up, your glossary terms and the names you gave the voices go with the text; for
      a series’ progress, the write-ups of its recordings with titles and dates. You withdraw your
      permission in Settings › Privacy.
    </p>

    {/* Un motore, un blocco: ognuno si toglie intero senza toccare gli altri. */}
    <div class="motore">
      <h4>On the phone</h4>
      <p>
        The transcription, the automatic title and, offline, the on-device engine and the proposed
        category: nothing leaves. The iPhone downloads the language models from Apple, with none of
        your data.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="motore">
      <h4>Apple Intelligence (Private Cloud Compute)</h4>
      <p>
        When the phone is online, the text goes to Apple’s Private Cloud Compute servers, which
        write the write-up: it is part of Apple Intelligence. Apple uses this data only to answer,
        doesn’t keep it, nobody at Apple can see it, and independent experts can verify this. If you
        ask to translate a transcription, they receive it to translate it; the translation stays on
        your iPhone. It also suggests a recording’s category, from the start of the write-up. You can
        turn it off in Settings › Privacy.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="motore">
      <h4>Minta Cloud</h4>
      <p>
        The text (or, for Whisper, a compressed copy of the audio) goes to Minta’s server in
        Frankfurt, hosted by Amazon Web Services, which passes it to the provider doing the work: <strong>Anthropic</strong> (Claude)
        writes the write-ups; <strong>Groq</strong> transcribes with Whisper and, rarely,
        writes a backup write-up when Anthropic is unavailable.
        They work on Minta’s behalf, in the United States, under the EU Standard Contractual Clauses, and
        don’t use the data to train their models: Groq keeps nothing, Anthropic deletes
        everything within 30 days. To show how many credits a write-up would use, Minta sends its
        server only the transcription’s length (or the recording’s duration), its language and the
        type you picked, never the text.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="motore">
      <h4>Podcasts</h4>
      <p>
        For a podcast you import, the phone asks Apple for the show’s details and downloads the
        feed, transcript and audio from the servers that host the podcast: they see the phone’s
        internet address, as with any podcast app. For an older episode its title also goes to
        Apple’s public search, and if you paste a web page’s link the phone reads that page.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="motore">
      <h4>iCloud sync</h4>
      <p>
        Off until you turn it on. Your recordings go to <strong>your own</strong> iCloud, at
        Apple, not to Minta’s server. The texts are end-to-end encrypted; Apple keeps the audio
        like your other iCloud data. A podcast’s audio doesn’t go to iCloud: it downloads again from
        its link. Turning it off deletes nothing: what is in iCloud you delete from the iCloud
        settings.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>Minta Cloud account</h3>
    <p>
      You sign in with Sign in with Apple: Minta gets only a code Apple creates, no name and no
      email. Apple handles payments: Minta never sees your payment details. It gets only your App
      Store country, which sets a pack’s credits, and doesn’t keep it.
    </p>

    <h3>What Minta’s server keeps, and for how long</h3>
    <dl class="conservazione">
      <div>
        <dt>Audio and texts</dt>
        <dd>
          Never stored. The result stays in memory for at most 15 minutes, to recover a request
          interrupted by iOS.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Credit, free trial, charges and purchases, tied to your Sign in with Apple code</dt>
        <dd>While you have the account; gone from backups within 24 hours of deleting it.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          A random support code, shown in Settings › Minta Cloud: if you include it when you
          write to support, it is used only to find your account
        </dt>
        <dd>While you have the account; deleted from backups within 24 hours of deleting the account.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Date and time of your yes to Minta Cloud (or of its withdrawal), with the version of the text you read</dt>
        <dd>While you have the account; gone from backups within 24 hours of deleting it.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Apple’s code of a purchase refunded before Minta credited it, not linked to the account</dt>
        <dd>1 year, so it isn’t credited.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Technical logs (durations, lengths, error codes, app version; never texts, audio, names or emails)</dt>
        <dd>30 days.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          After you delete the account: accounting records no longer linked to you, with Apple’s
          transaction code for refunds
        </dt>
        <dd>10 years (tax obligations).</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>After you delete the account: a one-way code of your Apple identifier</dt>
        <dd>Only to avoid a second free trial.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p>
      <strong>Why we process it.</strong> To give you the service you ask for (contract);
      accounting records and the date of your yes because the law requires it; the trial’s
      anti-abuse code and the refunded purchases’ codes for our legitimate interest in preventing
      abuse.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>iPhone permissions.</strong> Microphone to record, notifications for alerts, Live Activities to show on the Lock Screen the
      recording in progress or being worked on, with its name (it stays on the phone).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Your rights.</strong> You can ask to see, correct, delete or receive your data,
      restrict or object to its use, by writing to <a href="mailto:dan@trova.me">dan@trova.me</a>;
      you withdraw your yes to Minta Cloud whenever you like in Settings › Privacy; you delete your
      account yourself in the app (Settings › Minta Cloud; any remaining credit is lost). You can also
      complain to a data protection authority, such as Italy’s Garante (<a
        href="https://www.garanteprivacy.it">garanteprivacy.it</a
      >).
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Minta is not meant for children.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If any of this changes, this page changes with it, and the date below changes too.
    </p>

    <p class="stato">Last updated: {aggiornata}</p>
  </div>
</Base>
